R["«O74»"] = "H4 is partly rejected. The calibration slope is 1.027 (CI 0.985 to 1.070, p = 0.21), so the forecasts are not too spread out or too compressed. But the intercept is 0.319 and the total is 32.2% low: in every forecast decile actual units are 1.41 to 1.61 times the forecast. This is the known cost of a log-scale loss. The nominal 80% interval covered 71.5% (CI 70.7% to 72.4%); the conformal adjustment of 0.4 units lifted coverage to 77.7% (CI 76.9% to 78.5%) but still fell short of 80%, because the validation period differs from the test period. Use the model for shares, and rescale the volume with a calibration factor or the Poisson model."
R["«O75»"] = "H5 is rejected (Kendall Tau p-values are below 1e-4 for every forecast). Across 180 combinations the champion gives Pearson r = 0.876 (CI 0.829 to 0.906) and Tau = 0.709 (CI 0.702 to 0.761), against the naive 0.634 and 0.527. The Poisson linear model is nearly as good (r = 0.843, Tau = 0.697), so for combination-level buying a simple model is competitive. The top-20% capture is 42.0% for the champion and 42.3% for the Poisson linear model, against 36.3% for the naive forecast and a best possible 44.9%. The oracle's point value (0.448) sits below its own bootstrap interval (0.458 to 0.514): the percentile bootstrap on a ranking statistic is biased, so read these capture intervals loosely."
R["«O76»"] = "H6 is rejected. The naive visibility coefficient is 1.648 (CI 1.590 to 1.707) against a true 0.50, more than three times too large, because visibility partly reflects hidden style quality. With the quality term it is 0.455 (CI 0.420 to 0.489), still slightly below 0.50 (z = -2.6). The discount effect is recovered either way (0.023 vs 0.022; +10 points of discount multiplies units by 1.25, the true value). The promotion effect is 0.19 (true 0.22), and cannibalisation effects are small and noisy. By shuffling feature groups, merchandising matters most (wMAPE +0.206), then attributes (+0.127), age (+0.042), seasonality (+0.010), promotion calendar (+0.008) and cannibalisation (+0.004). Because merchandising is so strong, a forecast made before the plan is fixed will be worse."
R["«O77»"] = "H7 is rejected. On the same rows (age 4 weeks or more, naive wMAPE 0.979) the cold-start model has wMAPE 0.608; one early week gives 0.538 (gain 0.070, CI 0.061 to 0.080), two give 0.520 and four give 0.499 (gain 0.109, CI 0.099 to 0.121). Most of the gain arrives in the first week. In the buying simulation, a forecast-proportional split lifts pooled sell-through from 57.7% to 73.7%, a gain of 16.0 points (CI 14.8 to 17.2, relative +27.8%), cuts the lost-sales share of demand from 36.6% to 19.0% and the unsold share of stock from 42.3% to 26.3%. The oracle reaches 87.4%, so a perfect forecast would add another 13.7 points. The equal split is a weak benchmark; the paper's own gains of 7% and 10% are against its own baseline. To detect a 7% relative gain with 80% power, a live test needs about 1,095 styles per arm (537 for 10%, 2,145 for 5%)."
R["«O9»"] = "In the base case the forecast-based allocation earns **₹233,237 more profit per style** (95% CI ₹195,298 to ₹271,011), which is 58% of the gain a perfect forecast would give (₹400,111 vs ₹166,874 base profit per style, ₹565,821 for the oracle). Scaled to 50,000 styles a year, that is about **₹700 crore at 60% adoption**, with a worst case of ₹188 crore (45% liquidation recovery, lower interval end, 30% adoption) and a best case of ₹1,629 crore. The scenarios move profit mainly through liquidation value and adoption, both assumptions. The base case is an upper bound because the comparison is an equal split, which no real buyer uses."
R["«O10»"] = """**Recommendation:** use an attribute-and-plan gradient boosting forecast to split the buying budget across new styles and attribute combinations. Pilot it on a small set of brands before a wider rollout. Do not use it to set the total volume until it is rescaled (it ran 32% low), and do not read its merchandising coefficients as causal.

**Why:** the forecast cuts the error of a naive average out of time (wMAPE 0.597 vs 0.870), ranks attribute combinations well (Tau 0.71), and in the simulated plan lifts sell-through by 16.0 points over an equal split. Random-split validation would have overstated the quality by 0.03 to 0.05.

**Decision rule:**
- Go: in the live test, the forecast arm's sell-through beats the buyer-plan arm by at least 5% relative, with the 95% interval above zero, over at least 1,095 styles per arm (7% effect) or 2,145 (5%).
- Rescale: if the total bias is worse than 10% in either direction or the slope is outside 0.9 to 1.1, rescale or switch to the Poisson-loss model, which had a smaller bias (-13.7%).
- Stop and retrain: if monthly wMAPE exceeds the naive baseline minus 0.15, or interval coverage falls below 70%.
- Refresh with early sales after week 1, which cuts wMAPE by about 0.07.

**KPIs:** wMAPE and bias by article type and launch cohort; calibration slope; 80% interval coverage; sell-through at four weeks and at twelve weeks; lost-sales and unsold-stock shares; liquidation recovery per ₹ of cost; share of buying decisions that followed the forecast."""
